<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotubanamá National Park</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parque Nacional del Este</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotubanama">Cotubanamá</a> National Park, formerly the Parque Nacional del Este, is a protected area in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> that includes Saona Island. Created in 1975 and later renamed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chief Cotubanamá, it protects dry forest, mangroves and coastal reefs. Its limestone caves hold many Taíno pictographs and petroglyphs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotubanama-national-park.jpg" alt="Cotubanamá National Park" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS006-E-35895_-_View_of_Dominican_Republic_(cropped_and_rotated).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The park lies on the lower south-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, in the provinces of La Altagracia and La Romana, joining the coast around La Romana-Bayahibe and Boca de Yuma. It includes Saona Island, across the Catuano Strait, and the small Isla Catalinita. Much of the ground is limestone and coral, through which underground currents connect with the sea, and the coast is lined with mangroves, beaches and cliffs.</p><p>A study made before the park was declared found over 539 plant species, more than 50 of them endemic, among them mahogany, sea grape and guayiga. Its animals include the rhinoceros iguana, the hutia and the solenodon, more than 300 species of birds such as the Hispaniolan parrot, and on the coast coral reefs, manatees and dolphins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1492 the island was divided into five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chiefdoms, and the park lies in that of Higüey, ruled at the time of the Spanish conquest by the cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotubanama">Cotubanamá</a>. After the Spanish conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a> had the caciques of Saona Island killed, Cotubanamá led a rebellion. A truce failed after Spanish soldiers under Martin de Villaman committed atrocities against the Taíno, and a war of eight to nine months followed. Cotubanamá fled to Saona, was captured by a soldier, Juan López, and was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on the orders of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a> in 1504.</p><p>The park was declared by Decree No. 1311 in 1975, and in 2014 the National Congress gave it the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> in place of Parque Nacional del Este.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The park protects more than 20 ceremonial plazas, over eight indigenous cemeteries and hundreds of caves and sinkholes. Caves such as Jose Maria, Ramoncito and Berna hold thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> pictographs and petroglyphs, while the freshwater pools of the Padre Nuestro complex and the spring of Manantial de la Aleta, where many intact ceramics have been found, supplied drinking water. The park also includes a buffer zone of 12,000 hectares around its boundaries, and by 2021 it had been admitted to the Specially Protected Areas and Wildlife protocol of the Cartagena Convention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cotubanamá National Park” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caves such as those of the present-day <b>Cotubanamá National Park</b> hold Taíno rock art.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-higuey">Cacicazgo of Higüey</a></li></ul></section>
